Preface 
 
 
The political and so cio-economic imperatives of moving 
any country forward and planning for national prosperity 
require comprehensive data on the country’s human 
resource potentials.  The total population of Nigeria was 
enumerated during 2006 Population and Housing C ensus 
of March 21-27, 2006.  The Provisional result of the 2006 
Population and Housing Census was released in December 
of the same year and published in Federal Government 
Official Gazette N umber 4, Volume 94 of January 19, 
2007.  The Final result was publish ed in Gazette Number 
2, Volume 96 of 2 nd February, 2009.  The results present 
the total number of persons in each of the states and Abuja 
by gender.  The gazettes have since been disseminated to 
all stakeholders and the general public. 
This publication provides a breakdown of the state’s total 
population by their component Local Government Areas 
(LGAs).  Data on land size (as provided by the Office of 
the Surveyor General  of the Federation ) and the grouping 
of LGAs by Senatorial Districts (as provided by INEC) is  
also presented.  Additional information is also provided to 
assist users in appreciating and understanding the data 
presented in a wider context. 
More data will be generated and released to the public by 
the Commission. You are invited to v isit our websit e and 
to forward request to the Commission for any specific table 
not seen. 
The questions on how many we are in Nigeria should now 
be satisfactorily put to rest with the successful conduct of 
1991 and 2006 population censuses.  The nation now has 
comparable pop ulation census results to facilitate the 
provision of requisite data for national and sectoral 
planning, presently and in the future. 
The National Population Commission remains grateful to 
persons and organizations that made the 2006 Population 
and Housing Census a huge success. 
 
 
Chief Samu’ila Danko Makama, CON 
Chairman 
National Population Commission 
 
